{"name":"Tornado","hex":"#d1d3cf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tornado","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A violent wind in the form of a mobile, rapidly rotating, funnel cloud that has contacted the ground."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To sweep through something violently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tornado"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tornado","blocks":[{"text":"A tornado, also known as a twister, is a rapidly rotating column of air that extends vertically from the surface of the Earth to the base of a cumulonimbus or cumulus cloud. Tornadoes are often visible in the form of a condensation funnel originating from the cloud base, with a cloud of rotating debris and dust close to the ground. Most tornadoes have wind speeds less than 180 kilometers per hour, are about 80 meters across, and travel several kilometers before dissipating. The most extreme tornadoes can attain wind speeds of more than 480 kilometers per hour (300 mph), can be more than 3 kilometers (2 mi) in diameter, and can stay on the ground for more than 100 km (62 mi)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/F5_tornado_Elie_Manitoba_2007.jpg/330px-F5_tornado_Elie_Manitoba_2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tornado","wikidata":"Q8081"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/AllTdots.png/500px-AllTdots.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/AllTdots.png/1280px-AllTdots.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/AllTdots.png/500px-AllTdots.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tornadoes in the US, 1950–2013, highest F-scale on top, source NOAA Storm Prediction Center.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAllTdots.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/A_tornado_near_Anadarko%2C_Oklahoma%2C_on_May_3%2C_1999.jpg/500px-A_tornado_near_Anadarko%2C_Oklahoma%2C_on_May_3%2C_1999.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/A_tornado_near_Anadarko%2C_Oklahoma%2C_on_May_3%2C_1999.jpg/500px-A_tornado_near_Anadarko%2C_Oklahoma%2C_on_May_3%2C_1999.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A tornado near Anadarko, Oklahoma, 1999. The funnel is the thin tube reaching from the cloud to the ground. The lower part of this tornado is surrounded by a translucent dust cloud, kicked up by the tornado's strong winds at the surface. The wind of the tornado has a much wider radius than the funnel itself.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_tornado_near_Anadarko%2C_Oklahoma%2C_on_May_3%2C_1999.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Stovepipe_Tornado_near_Yuma%2C_Colorado.jpg/500px-Stovepipe_Tornado_near_Yuma%2C_Colorado.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Stovepipe_Tornado_near_Yuma%2C_Colorado.jpg/1280px-Stovepipe_Tornado_near_Yuma%2C_Colorado.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Stovepipe_Tornado_near_Yuma%2C_Colorado.jpg/500px-Stovepipe_Tornado_near_Yuma%2C_Colorado.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStovepipe_Tornado_near_Yuma%2C_Colorado.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Rope_tornado_in_LaGrange%2C_Wyoming.jpg/500px-Rope_tornado_in_LaGrange%2C_Wyoming.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_